WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.142 --> 00:00:08.002
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.002 --> 00:00:10.062
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.062 --> 00:00:12.942
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.952 --> 00:00:14.722
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:17.292
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.302 --> 00:00:19.332
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.332 --> 00:00:21.122
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.572 --> 00:00:24.042
I’m Alastair Budge, and this is

00:00:24.042 --> 00:00:26.782
the third and final part of our

00:00:26.782 --> 00:00:30.112
mini-series, Tales from the Wild West.

00:00:30.482 --> 00:00:33.952
In part one, we looked at Wyatt Earp, and

00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:36.932
the gap between the man and the legend.

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:40.212
In part two, we met Bass Reeves:

00:00:40.492 --> 00:00:43.312
the most feared lawman in the West.

00:00:43.682 --> 00:00:46.642
And today, we are going to look at the

00:00:46.642 --> 00:00:49.382
West from a completely different angle.

00:00:49.752 --> 00:00:52.162
Not cowboys, not lawmen, not

00:00:52.232 --> 00:00:54.722
outlaws, but the people who were

00:00:54.722 --> 00:00:56.902
there before everyone else arrived.

00:00:57.292 --> 00:01:00.232
And the story of one man in particular:

00:01:00.592 --> 00:01:02.952
Geronimo, the so-called “worst

00:01:02.972 --> 00:01:05.742
Indian who ever lived”, a man who was

00:01:05.742 --> 00:01:08.722
feared as a murderer, celebrated as a

00:01:08.722 --> 00:01:12.172
warrior, displayed as a curiosity, and

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:14.742
remembered as a symbol of resistance.

00:01:15.052 --> 00:01:17.172
So, let’s not waste a minute,

00:01:17.532 --> 00:01:19.471
and get right into it.

00:01:20.952 --> 00:01:23.215
It is March, 1851.

00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:26.502
A group of Apache men, women, and

00:01:26.502 --> 00:01:29.262
children are camped near the Mexican

00:01:29.262 --> 00:01:32.632
town of Janos, in the state of Chihuahua.

00:01:33.902 --> 00:01:36.892
The men have gone into town to trade.

00:01:37.292 --> 00:01:39.582
They leave their families at the camp.

00:01:40.672 --> 00:01:43.822
While they are gone, Mexican soldiers

00:01:44.162 --> 00:01:46.679
under the command of Colonel José

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:49.772
María Carrasco ride into the camp.

00:01:50.462 --> 00:01:52.882
The soldiers kill the women.

00:01:53.262 --> 00:01:54.372
They kill the children.

00:01:54.822 --> 00:01:56.332
They kill the elderly.

00:01:57.492 --> 00:02:00.752
When the trading party returns, they find

00:02:00.782 --> 00:02:03.522
the lifeless bodies of their loved ones.

00:02:04.752 --> 00:02:08.352
One young man finds his wife, his

00:02:08.392 --> 00:02:10.712
three children, and his mother.

00:02:11.492 --> 00:02:13.382
All of them, slaughtered.

00:02:14.941 --> 00:02:17.511
That man is a twenty-one-year-old

00:02:17.761 --> 00:02:19.181
named Goyaałé.

00:02:20.161 --> 00:02:22.431
He stands at the edge of the camp

00:02:22.781 --> 00:02:25.071
and looks at what has been done.

00:02:25.881 --> 00:02:27.711
He never speaks about what

00:02:27.781 --> 00:02:30.281
exactly he felt in that moment.

00:02:30.721 --> 00:02:33.266
But what we do know is that,

00:02:33.273 --> 00:02:35.671
there and then, he made a

00:02:35.681 --> 00:02:38.911
solemn vow to avenge his family.

00:02:39.531 --> 00:02:41.671
And he would spend the next

00:02:41.711 --> 00:02:43.881
thirty-five years doing it.

00:02:45.241 --> 00:02:49.081
This man, Goyaałé, was born in June

00:02:49.291 --> 00:02:53.071
1829, in what is now the borderlands

00:02:53.301 --> 00:02:56.061
between Arizona and New Mexico.

00:02:57.051 --> 00:02:59.611
He was Chiricahua Apache, one

00:02:59.611 --> 00:03:01.631
of several Apache groups who had

00:03:01.631 --> 00:03:03.921
lived in this area for centuries.

00:03:04.731 --> 00:03:07.851
Now, the Apache were not a single

00:03:08.011 --> 00:03:10.081
unified nation in the way that

00:03:10.091 --> 00:03:12.931
European nations understood nationhood.

00:03:13.511 --> 00:03:15.061
They were a collection of

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:17.991
bands, loosely connected by

00:03:18.021 --> 00:03:20.281
language and shared territory.

00:03:20.941 --> 00:03:22.611
They were skilled at moving

00:03:22.611 --> 00:03:24.441
quickly over difficult ground,

00:03:24.781 --> 00:03:27.031
and at living with very little.

00:03:27.471 --> 00:03:29.251
The desert and the mountains,

00:03:29.481 --> 00:03:31.031
these were their home.

00:03:32.261 --> 00:03:35.371
And to clarify, the Apache were

00:03:35.371 --> 00:03:38.161
not the “Indians” of Western films.

00:03:38.306 --> 00:03:40.931
There were no feathered headdresses,

00:03:40.951 --> 00:03:44.271
no vast plains, no buffalo herds.

00:03:44.701 --> 00:03:46.191
They were desert people,

00:03:46.551 --> 00:03:49.021
mountain people, a nomadic people

00:03:49.221 --> 00:03:51.021
who moved with the seasons.

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:55.241
They had lived on this land for centuries,

00:03:55.441 --> 00:03:58.521
but foreigners had been encroaching on it.

00:03:59.841 --> 00:04:02.201
First the Spanish, in the 17th

00:04:02.201 --> 00:04:05.341
century, and the Mexicans after

00:04:05.351 --> 00:04:08.121
independence from Spain in 1821.

00:04:09.247 --> 00:04:12.547
As such, Apache life was dominated

00:04:12.587 --> 00:04:15.924
by raids and counter raids: Apache

00:04:15.924 --> 00:04:18.347
warriors would attack Spanish and then

00:04:18.497 --> 00:04:21.597
Mexican camps, stealing food and horses,

00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:23.667
before retreating to the mountains.

00:04:24.247 --> 00:04:25.867
And the Spanish, and then the

00:04:25.877 --> 00:04:28.237
Mexicans, would chase after them.

00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:32.307
This was simply how life worked,

00:04:32.477 --> 00:04:34.927
a violent cycle of raiding.

00:04:36.387 --> 00:04:40.417
And Goyaałé grew up inside that cycle.

00:04:40.807 --> 00:04:41.427
Fighting.

00:04:41.857 --> 00:04:42.457
Raiding.

00:04:42.847 --> 00:04:44.337
Retreating to the mountains.

00:04:45.717 --> 00:04:50.363
And this was further complicated in 1848,

00:04:50.807 --> 00:04:52.957
when the war between the United States

00:04:52.987 --> 00:04:56.387
and Mexico ended with an American victory.

00:04:57.417 --> 00:05:01.057
This meant that a new international border

00:05:01.427 --> 00:05:04.647
now cut straight through Apache territory.

00:05:05.397 --> 00:05:07.947
What had been Mexican land, and

00:05:08.162 --> 00:05:10.157
Apache land long before that,

00:05:10.637 --> 00:05:13.347
was now American, and American

00:05:13.347 --> 00:05:15.657
settlers were beginning to move in.

00:05:16.977 --> 00:05:18.797
Three years later came

00:05:18.807 --> 00:05:20.777
the massacre at Janos.

00:05:21.137 --> 00:05:21.847
His wife.

00:05:22.247 --> 00:05:23.017
His children.

00:05:23.657 --> 00:05:24.237
His mother.

00:05:24.927 --> 00:05:26.737
And the solemn promise

00:05:26.947 --> 00:05:28.637
to avenge their death.

00:05:29.877 --> 00:05:32.817
Now, there are accounts that after

00:05:32.837 --> 00:05:35.577
the massacre, Goyaałé went alone

00:05:35.607 --> 00:05:38.147
into the forest to mourn his family.

00:05:38.977 --> 00:05:41.537
And there, he heard a voice.

00:05:42.357 --> 00:05:45.537
The voice said, “No gun will ever

00:05:45.537 --> 00:05:47.867
kill you. I will take the bullets

00:05:48.007 --> 00:05:49.857
from the guns of the Mexicans

00:05:50.087 --> 00:05:52.587
and I will guide your arrows.”

00:05:54.397 --> 00:05:56.847
And for decades, it would seem

00:05:56.847 --> 00:05:58.767
as if the voice had been right.

00:05:59.977 --> 00:06:02.067
Now, what followed was not

00:06:02.067 --> 00:06:04.307
a war in any formal sense.

00:06:04.807 --> 00:06:06.407
It was something more

00:06:06.477 --> 00:06:09.187
personal: a quest for revenge.

00:06:10.147 --> 00:06:13.247
Goyaałé could not identify the specific

00:06:13.247 --> 00:06:15.167
soldiers who had killed his family.

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:17.867
So he did what Apache

00:06:17.867 --> 00:06:19.247
warriors had always done.

00:06:19.857 --> 00:06:20.987
He raided.

00:06:21.835 --> 00:06:23.187
But these raids were different.

00:06:23.537 --> 00:06:26.107
He was not after horses or cattle.

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:28.607
He was going back to the same

00:06:28.607 --> 00:06:31.127
region, attacking the same Mexican

00:06:31.127 --> 00:06:33.757
military forces, again and again.

00:06:35.247 --> 00:06:38.826
There is no way of knowing how many

00:06:38.826 --> 00:06:40.417
people he killed, and to quote his

00:06:40.417 --> 00:06:42.867
later biography, “I have killed many

00:06:42.867 --> 00:06:45.047
Mexicans; I do not know how many,

00:06:45.397 --> 00:06:47.567
for frequently I did not count them.”

00:06:48.757 --> 00:06:51.747
These raids were fast, violent,

00:06:51.947 --> 00:06:53.767
and hard to defend against.

00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:57.277
A small band would strike at night or

00:06:57.277 --> 00:07:00.127
at dawn, take what they needed, kill

00:07:00.197 --> 00:07:03.017
anyone who got in their way, and be gone

00:07:03.227 --> 00:07:05.587
before a response could be organised.

00:07:06.927 --> 00:07:09.627
To Mexicans and later Americans on

00:07:09.627 --> 00:07:12.717
the frontier, Goyaałé, or should I say

00:07:12.727 --> 00:07:15.657
Geronimo, he was not a freedom fighter.

00:07:16.017 --> 00:07:17.097
He was a terror.

00:07:18.417 --> 00:07:21.057
As for the name Geronimo, there are

00:07:21.057 --> 00:07:23.537
several theories about where it came from.

00:07:24.167 --> 00:07:26.517
Perhaps it was the Mexican soldiers’

00:07:26.527 --> 00:07:28.767
best attempt at pronouncing his name,

00:07:29.017 --> 00:07:31.507
Goyaałé, a name that I am no doubt

00:07:31.507 --> 00:07:33.327
slightly butchering here as well.

00:07:34.307 --> 00:07:36.887
The most common view is that Mexican

00:07:36.887 --> 00:07:39.627
soldiers would cry out to a saint

00:07:39.657 --> 00:07:41.827
called San Geronimo during the raids.

00:07:42.247 --> 00:07:44.917
And eventually, Goyaałé’s own people

00:07:45.137 --> 00:07:47.417
decided this was a good name to call him.

00:07:48.397 --> 00:07:50.557
However it came about, the name

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:52.567
stuck, and became the name by

00:07:52.567 --> 00:07:54.207
which the world would know him.

00:07:55.247 --> 00:07:57.637
And with each raid, each

00:07:57.717 --> 00:08:00.287
skirmish, his reputation grew.

00:08:00.957 --> 00:08:03.737
He fought in the desert, in temperatures

00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:06.247
that could reach fifty degrees Celsius.

00:08:06.717 --> 00:08:08.997
He fought outnumbered, survived

00:08:08.997 --> 00:08:10.886
ambushes that he should not have

00:08:10.886 --> 00:08:13.647
survived, and escaped situations

00:08:13.847 --> 00:08:15.777
nobody could quite explain.

00:08:16.937 --> 00:08:20.057
On one occasion, he ran out of arrows

00:08:20.197 --> 00:08:21.977
while fighting Mexican soldiers.

00:08:22.407 --> 00:08:25.617
So he charged directly at them through

00:08:25.617 --> 00:08:28.237
the gunfire, zigzagging, closing the

00:08:28.237 --> 00:08:31.167
distance, and killed them with his knife.

00:08:31.867 --> 00:08:34.007
He came out unscathed.

00:08:35.067 --> 00:08:37.427
Among his own people, there were stories

00:08:37.647 --> 00:08:40.327
that he had gifts that went beyond

00:08:40.477 --> 00:08:43.497
ordinary courage: that he had visions,

00:08:43.547 --> 00:08:46.347
that he could not be hit by bullets, that

00:08:46.347 --> 00:08:50.316
he could sense danger before it arrived.

00:08:50.336 --> 00:08:54.077
Still, even after years of violence and

00:08:54.267 --> 00:08:57.177
the seeming thirst for revenge, there

00:08:57.177 --> 00:09:00.057
were moments when peace seemed possible.

00:09:01.187 --> 00:09:05.087
In 1873, the Apache and the Mexicans

00:09:05.127 --> 00:09:08.087
met and agreed to a peace treaty.

00:09:08.567 --> 00:09:11.027
Geronimo was among those present.

00:09:11.937 --> 00:09:14.757
Once the terms were signed, the Mexicans

00:09:15.017 --> 00:09:17.237
brought out the mezcal to celebrate.

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:21.037
But when they were drunk, Mexican soldiers

00:09:21.327 --> 00:09:24.737
attacked and killed twenty Apaches.

00:09:25.277 --> 00:09:28.207
Somehow, Geronimo managed to escape.

00:09:29.747 --> 00:09:32.107
As you might imagine, from that point

00:09:32.137 --> 00:09:36.077
on he was not keen to negotiate, and the

00:09:36.077 --> 00:09:39.337
cycle of raiding and revenge resumed.

00:09:40.467 --> 00:09:43.347
What’s more, by this time, by the

00:09:43.507 --> 00:09:47.357
1870s, Geronimo had a new and more

00:09:47.507 --> 00:09:49.847
powerful enemy: the United States.

00:09:51.027 --> 00:09:54.077
After the Mexican-American war, American

00:09:54.077 --> 00:09:57.357
settlers, soldiers and officials had begun

00:09:57.457 --> 00:10:00.157
pressing deeper into Apache territory

00:10:01.377 --> 00:10:04.607
Their objective was to subdue the Apaches,

00:10:05.107 --> 00:10:08.907
and the tool to do so was the reservation.

00:10:10.166 --> 00:10:12.557
The reservation, in the American

00:10:12.557 --> 00:10:15.077
context, was a defined area of

00:10:15.077 --> 00:10:18.067
land set aside for a Native people

00:10:18.067 --> 00:10:20.497
to live on, which was typically a

00:10:20.507 --> 00:10:22.792
fraction of their previous territory.

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:27.277
The policy was assimilation: make

00:10:27.376 --> 00:10:29.407
them farmers, teach their children

00:10:29.417 --> 00:10:32.797
English, and eventually fold them

00:10:32.797 --> 00:10:34.817
into the new American nation.

00:10:36.267 --> 00:10:38.007
It rarely worked out that way.

00:10:38.677 --> 00:10:40.717
Reservation land was often

00:10:40.757 --> 00:10:42.357
unsuitable for agriculture.

00:10:42.697 --> 00:10:44.417
The promised food and

00:10:44.417 --> 00:10:46.357
supplies rarely arrived.

00:10:46.877 --> 00:10:48.617
The agents appointed to manage the

00:10:48.617 --> 00:10:51.087
reservations were frequently corrupt.

00:10:52.577 --> 00:10:54.977
The San Carlos Reservation, where

00:10:55.167 --> 00:10:58.247
Geronimo’s people were sent in 1876,

00:10:58.647 --> 00:11:02.517
had earned a nickname: Hell’s 40 Acres.

00:11:03.097 --> 00:11:06.217
It was an arid, disease-ridden wasteland,

00:11:06.587 --> 00:11:10.344
overcrowded and desperately undersupplied.

00:11:11.723 --> 00:11:15.407
Several times between the 1870s and

00:11:15.526 --> 00:11:19.487
late 1880s, Geronimo broke out of San

00:11:19.487 --> 00:11:21.907
Carlos and returned to the mountains.

00:11:22.547 --> 00:11:23.937
He continued the raiding

00:11:24.237 --> 00:11:26.477
in Mexico and in Arizona.

00:11:27.277 --> 00:11:29.277
He found himself being chased by

00:11:29.397 --> 00:11:31.197
thousands of American soldiers

00:11:31.457 --> 00:11:33.707
and hundreds of Mexican troops.

00:11:34.617 --> 00:11:37.797
His skill at escaping capture was

00:11:37.797 --> 00:11:40.007
such that the US Army sent one of

00:11:40.007 --> 00:11:42.397
its most experienced commanders to

00:11:42.397 --> 00:11:45.067
bring him in: General George Crook.

00:11:46.177 --> 00:11:49.477
In 1883, after tracking him high

00:11:49.527 --> 00:11:51.837
up into the mountains, and sending

00:11:51.847 --> 00:11:54.367
Apache scouts ahead to persuade

00:11:54.397 --> 00:11:57.247
Geronimo to negotiate, Crook and

00:11:57.247 --> 00:12:00.577
Geronimo finally met face-to-face.

00:12:01.877 --> 00:12:03.867
The conversation, reportedly,

00:12:03.997 --> 00:12:05.867
was blunt and factual.

00:12:06.837 --> 00:12:08.427
Crook told him the

00:12:08.427 --> 00:12:10.757
situation was unsustainable.

00:12:11.797 --> 00:12:15.117
Geronimo, who was no fool, agreed

00:12:15.167 --> 00:12:16.787
to return to the reservation.

00:12:17.977 --> 00:12:20.067
He did, and he stayed,

00:12:20.307 --> 00:12:21.317
for a while at least.

00:12:22.157 --> 00:12:25.227
Then the conditions deteriorated again.

00:12:25.837 --> 00:12:27.237
The food rations the government

00:12:27.237 --> 00:12:29.417
had promised failed to arrive.

00:12:29.887 --> 00:12:31.307
The land they had been

00:12:31.337 --> 00:12:33.764
allocated was barely farmable.

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:36.617
It was miserable, an open prison.

00:12:37.917 --> 00:12:40.127
Then an Apache leader was

00:12:40.147 --> 00:12:42.227
arrested on dubious grounds.

00:12:43.007 --> 00:12:44.857
Geronimo clearly believed a

00:12:44.867 --> 00:12:46.817
similar fate might await him,

00:12:47.327 --> 00:12:49.967
and he was safer out, than in.

00:12:51.247 --> 00:12:54.677
In 1885 he left again, taking a

00:12:54.707 --> 00:12:56.677
small band of Apaches with him.

00:12:57.827 --> 00:13:01.097
This last flight would last over a year.

00:13:02.107 --> 00:13:04.927
Five thousand American soldiers, which

00:13:05.077 --> 00:13:07.537
was roughly one fifth of the entire

00:13:07.547 --> 00:13:11.067
United States Army, and five hundred

00:13:11.077 --> 00:13:13.877
Mexican soldiers were deployed to catch

00:13:13.877 --> 00:13:17.437
them, just one man and his small band.

00:13:18.317 --> 00:13:20.287
And the raids continued.

00:13:20.697 --> 00:13:23.977
Again and again, tiny Apache bands

00:13:24.147 --> 00:13:26.757
were able to strike, disappear into

00:13:26.757 --> 00:13:29.007
the mountains, and evade forces

00:13:29.247 --> 00:13:33.156
many times larger than themselves.

00:13:33.176 --> 00:13:35.367
To give you a sense of the numbers here,

00:13:35.817 --> 00:13:39.447
one estimate has it that in just the final

00:13:39.477 --> 00:13:42.817
five months of Geronimo’s campaign, a

00:13:42.847 --> 00:13:46.327
band of sixteen warriors killed between

00:13:46.347 --> 00:13:49.417
five hundred and six hundred Mexicans.

00:13:51.137 --> 00:13:53.537
The number may well be exaggerated.

00:13:53.607 --> 00:13:56.277
But if it’s even half of that,

00:13:56.527 --> 00:13:59.007
it gives you an idea of what he

00:13:59.007 --> 00:14:00.607
and his band were capable of.

00:14:01.997 --> 00:14:06.037
But by the mid-1880s, Geronimo was in

00:14:06.037 --> 00:14:09.027
his mid-fifties, and the arithmetic of

00:14:09.027 --> 00:14:11.517
the situation had become impossible.

00:14:12.157 --> 00:14:14.667
The band he was leading had shrunk.

00:14:15.057 --> 00:14:16.687
His people were exhausted.

00:14:17.157 --> 00:14:19.757
Mexico and the United States were

00:14:19.757 --> 00:14:21.197
working together to find him.

00:14:21.847 --> 00:14:23.767
There was nowhere left to go.

00:14:25.317 --> 00:14:28.987
By early 1886, Crook had tracked

00:14:28.997 --> 00:14:31.307
Geronimo deep into the mountains of

00:14:31.307 --> 00:14:34.897
Mexico, and the two men met again.

00:14:35.897 --> 00:14:38.157
Geronimo agreed to surrender.

00:14:38.557 --> 00:14:41.397
But that same night, a soldier

00:14:41.577 --> 00:14:44.247
from Crook’s column sold the band

00:14:44.247 --> 00:14:46.747
whiskey and told them they would

00:14:46.757 --> 00:14:49.417
be executed as soon as they crossed

00:14:49.417 --> 00:14:51.277
back into the United States.

00:14:51.997 --> 00:14:53.557
Geronimo fled.

00:14:54.067 --> 00:14:55.767
It was a deep humiliation for

00:14:55.767 --> 00:14:58.057
Crook, and he was replaced by

00:14:58.097 --> 00:15:00.897
another general called Nelson Miles.

00:15:02.307 --> 00:15:04.037
Miles was different.

00:15:04.447 --> 00:15:07.327
He was less patient, more conventional,

00:15:07.627 --> 00:15:10.477
and more interested in overwhelming

00:15:10.606 --> 00:15:13.286
force than in using Apache scouts.

00:15:14.957 --> 00:15:17.487
He pursued Geronimo with five

00:15:17.507 --> 00:15:19.107
thousand American soldiers.

00:15:19.817 --> 00:15:22.287
But it was to no avail.

00:15:22.607 --> 00:15:25.397
Miles, like Crook before him, was

00:15:25.437 --> 00:15:27.487
eventually forced to negotiate.

00:15:28.647 --> 00:15:31.137
He sent two officers ahead, along

00:15:31.137 --> 00:15:33.727
with a trusted Apache intermediary, to

00:15:33.727 --> 00:15:36.267
find Geronimo and propose a meeting.

00:15:37.337 --> 00:15:40.427
The message was simple: come and

00:15:40.427 --> 00:15:42.897
talk, or the pursuit continues

00:15:43.157 --> 00:15:45.297
until there is no one left.

00:15:46.717 --> 00:15:48.267
After several weeks of

00:15:48.277 --> 00:15:50.607
back-and-forth, Geronimo agreed.

00:15:51.847 --> 00:15:55.997
On the 4th of September, 1886, at a place

00:15:55.997 --> 00:15:58.557
called Skeleton Canyon in the mountains

00:15:58.567 --> 00:16:02.277
of southeastern Arizona, Geronimo came in

00:16:02.277 --> 00:16:05.227
from the mountains and gave up his rifle.

00:16:06.717 --> 00:16:09.117
He shook hands with General Nelson

00:16:09.117 --> 00:16:11.197
Miles of the United States Army.

00:16:11.937 --> 00:16:13.457
And surrendered.

00:16:14.857 --> 00:16:16.617
He was the last major leader

00:16:16.867 --> 00:16:18.357
of the Apache resistance.

00:16:18.947 --> 00:16:21.697
His surrender is often described as

00:16:21.697 --> 00:16:24.647
the end of the Indian Wars: the end,

00:16:25.077 --> 00:16:27.867
in some sense, of the entire era of

00:16:27.967 --> 00:16:30.187
large-scale armed Native American

00:16:30.187 --> 00:16:32.907
resistance to the conquest of their lands.

00:16:33.747 --> 00:16:35.717
He was fifty-seven years old.

00:16:36.647 --> 00:16:39.077
He had been fighting, more or less

00:16:39.097 --> 00:16:42.197
continuously, for thirty-five years.

00:16:43.357 --> 00:16:46.267
His band, by the time of the surrender,

00:16:46.867 --> 00:16:49.047
had shrunk to just thirty-eight

00:16:49.087 --> 00:16:51.897
people: men, women, and children.

00:16:52.587 --> 00:16:54.337
After his capture, many

00:16:54.337 --> 00:16:56.437
people wanted him executed.

00:16:57.217 --> 00:16:59.497
After all, he had led attacks that

00:16:59.507 --> 00:17:01.737
killed soldiers and civilians,

00:17:01.887 --> 00:17:04.037
and there were calls for him to

00:17:04.037 --> 00:17:06.827
be made an example of and hanged.

00:17:08.077 --> 00:17:09.367
It did not happen.

00:17:09.907 --> 00:17:12.957
The Army had classified Geronimo as

00:17:12.957 --> 00:17:15.507
a prisoner of war, not a criminal.

00:17:16.287 --> 00:17:18.187
Under the terms he had surrendered

00:17:18.187 --> 00:17:21.067
on, executing him was not an option.

00:17:22.217 --> 00:17:24.326
And there was a practical calculation

00:17:24.446 --> 00:17:27.897
too: if the Army hanged men who

00:17:27.897 --> 00:17:30.917
surrendered under terms, no future

00:17:30.977 --> 00:17:33.397
Apache leader would ever agree to talk.

00:17:34.167 --> 00:17:36.367
Dead, he would be a martyr.

00:17:36.877 --> 00:17:39.377
As a prisoner, he could be managed.

00:17:40.541 --> 00:17:42.950
Geronimo was led to believe that

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.700
he and his people would be briefly

00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:47.740
imprisoned and then allowed to

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:50.040
return to their land in Arizona.

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:54.270
This did not happen, and what happened

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:57.260
next was nothing short of betrayal.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:01.310
He and his band were loaded onto trains

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:04.130
and transported to a military fort

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.830
in Florida, thousands of kilometres

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.150
from everything they had ever known.

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:11.440
They were not the only ones.

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:14.910
The Apache scouts, the men of Geronimo’s

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:17.140
own people who had helped the US

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:20.230
Army track him down, they too were

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:23.040
sent to Florida as prisoners of war.

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:26.200
Now, to be sent thousands of

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:27.850
kilometres from home would

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:29.630
have been painful for anyone.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:33.760
But for the Chiricahua Apache, with their

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:36.360
particular connection to the desert, to

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:39.620
the land, it was a deeper kind of exile.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:43.980
Florida was humid, swampy, and

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:46.420
utterly alien, a different planet.

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:48.500
They were miserable, and

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.550
many died of disease.

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:53.760
After almost two years, the

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:55.390
group was moved to Alabama.

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:57.510
Then, eventually, to Fort

00:18:57.520 --> 00:18:59.380
Sill in Oklahoma Territory.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.850
Fort Sill was slightly better.

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:04.900
The climate was more familiar.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:06.880
The Apache were given their own

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:09.710
dwellings, allowed to farm small plots

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.480
of land, and to raise their families.

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.440
But still, they could not leave.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.940
It was a prison without the bars.

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:21.240
And as for Goyaałé, for

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:23.720
Geronimo, he tried to adapt.

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:25.050
He took up farming.

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:27.900
He converted to Christianity and

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:29.690
joined the church, only to be

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:32.150
expelled after repeated failed

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:34.060
promises to give up gambling.

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:38.130
And at the same time, to bring back it

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.260
to the wider theme of this mini-series,

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:43.090
the same myth-making machine that was

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:45.450
turning the Wild West into entertainment

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:48.280
was doing the same thing for Geronimo.

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:51.990
He had had an early taste of this

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:54.810
on the train journey to Fort Sill.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.650
At every station stop, crowds

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:00.980
had gathered to catch a glimpse

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:02.720
of the infamous Geronimo.

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:05.200
Tourists pressed against the

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:07.690
windows wanting a souvenir, some

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:10.540
memento of the great Apache warrior.

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.910
Like any good capitalist,

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:15.994
he was willing to oblige.

00:20:16.970 --> 00:20:19.560
He cut buttons from his coat and

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:22.390
sold them for twenty-five cents each.

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:25.050
When the train started again, he would

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.140
sew on new buttons, to be able to

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:29.190
repeat the process at the next stop.

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:33.060
He was still technically a prisoner

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:36.200
of war, but he realised he was also a

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:40.000
celebrity, a curiosity, a living relic.

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:42.800
He was displayed as one of the attractions

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:46.650
at the 1904 World’s Fair in St. Louis,

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:49.490
and he took the opportunity to sell

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:51.690
photographs and personal belongings.

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:53.960
He charged twenty-five cents for

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:56.200
his autograph and sold bows and

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:58.190
arrows that he had made himself,

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:00.060
or at least claimed that he had.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:03.290
People queued to see him, the man who

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:05.710
was advertised at fairs and exhibitions

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:08.450
as “the worst Indian who ever lived.”

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:11.310
It seems that he understood

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:12.950
the transaction perfectly.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:15.700
And he would give the people what they

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:18.040
wanted, for the right price, of course.

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:22.600
The following year, in 1905, he

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:25.140
was even invited to the inaugural

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:27.470
parade of President Theodore

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:29.613
Roosevelt in Washington DC.

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:33.700
Six Native American chiefs rode

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:35.530
together through the crowds.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:37.640
Geronimo was one of them.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:40.420
There are photographs of him in

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:43.060
full ceremonial dress, riding

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:45.290
down Pennsylvania Avenue past

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.330
thousands of cheering Americans.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:51.450
He agreed partly for the fee, but

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:53.610
also because he hoped to make his

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:55.700
case directly to the President.

00:21:56.670 --> 00:21:58.950
After all, remember, he was still a

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:02.290
prisoner of war, and he wanted to go home.

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:06.440
He met with Roosevelt and asked him

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:10.380
directly: let my people return to Arizona.

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:13.110
We have been punished for long enough.

00:22:14.644 --> 00:22:16.810
Roosevelt’s reply was blunt.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:18.160
No.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:21.610
“You killed many of my people,” he is

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:23.590
said to have told Geronimo. “You burned

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:26.110
villages. You were not good Indians.”

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:29.230
So Geronimo did the

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:30.880
only thing left to him.

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:33.860
He told his own story, and

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:35.720
published an autobiography.

00:22:37.180 --> 00:22:39.200
He dedicated it to Roosevelt.

00:22:39.860 --> 00:22:42.020
The dedication reads, in part:

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:44.360
“Because he has given me permission

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.100
to tell my story; because he is

00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:48.640
fair-minded and will cause my people

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:50.800
to receive justice in the future.”

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:54.030
Roosevelt read the book.

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:57.130
He still did not change his position.

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:01.880
As for Geronimo, he died on the 17th

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:05.240
of February, 1909, at Fort Sill.

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:08.270
He had fallen from his horse a

00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:10.150
few days earlier, had lain in

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:12.530
the cold all night before being

00:23:12.530 --> 00:23:15.420
found, and pneumonia had set in.

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:17.910
He was seventy-nine years old.

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:21.680
On his deathbed, he is reported

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:23.649
to have said, “I should never have

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:25.430
surrendered. I should have fought

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:27.670
until I was the last man alive.”

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:32.450
So, to wrap things up, the story

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.440
of the Wild West, or at least this

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:36.670
mini-series, does not end with

00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:39.120
a cowboy riding into the sunset.

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:41.970
It ends, perhaps, with an old man on

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:44.320
a horse, riding through the crowds

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:47.370
in Washington DC, dressed in the

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:49.860
ceremonial clothes of a people whose

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:52.760
land had been taken from them, cheered

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:55.240
by the same country that had taken it.

00:23:56.060 --> 00:23:59.200
In Wyatt Earp, we saw how the West

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:02.040
turned flawed men into legends.

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:05.180
In Bass Reeves, we saw how it could

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:08.040
ignore men who deserved to become legends.

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:11.660
And in Geronimo, we see the thing the

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:14.010
legend tried hardest not to look at:

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:17.330
that the winning of the West was also

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:19.650
the taking of someone else’s world.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.880
Geronimo was called many things:

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:26.290
a savage, a murderer, a warrior, a

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:29.810
celebrity, a prisoner, a relic, even

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:31.920
the worst Indian who ever lived.

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:34.990
But perhaps, more than anything,

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:37.770
he was a man who had seen his world

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:41.000
destroyed, and refused, for as long

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:43.579
as he possibly could, to accept it.

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:47.970
OK then, that is it for today’s

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:50.660
episode on Geronimo, and with that

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:52.200
comes an end to this three-part

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:55.010
mini-series on Tales from the Wild West.

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:57.140
I hope you’ve enjoyed it, and it’s been an

00:24:57.150 --> 00:24:59.360
interesting way to dive into the history

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:02.040
of the West in a slightly unusual way.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:04.410
As a reminder, just in case you missed

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:07.400
them, part one was on Wyatt Earp, the

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:09.490
legendary lawman, who wasn’t all that

00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:10.900
good at following the law himself.

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:12.840
Part two was on the true

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:14.570
legend that was Bass Reeves.

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:16.460
And part three, well, that’s

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:17.350
what you’ve just listened to.

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:19.950
You’ve been listening to English Learning

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:22.560
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:25.620
I’m Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:27.580
I’ll catch you in the next episode.

